rv.EN_ABSTRACT = [
    ('Objective: ', 'To determine how the use of parameter estimates in the observer and controller of a sensorless induction motor drive affects speed regulation accuracy, maximum current and stator copper-loss energy. '),
    ('Methods: ', 'Fixed-parameter control, observer-only adaptation, and direct and covariance-weighted controller updates were compared in 320 simulations. Forty additional control simulations used matched load process covariance. Physical parameter estimation accuracy was evaluated separately. '),
    ('Results: ', 'Under parameter variations and combined disturbances, the weighted update reduced speed error by 66.2–67.5% relative to the baseline, with mean individual current maxima changing by less than 1% and copper-loss energy increasing by 2.6–3.0%. Relative to observer-only adaptation, transmitting weighted parameter estimates to the controller reduced mean current maxima by 24.8–27.1% but increased speed error by 58.6–76.5%. The accuracy advantage over the baseline persisted with matched load process covariance. Terminal mean absolute relative stator resistance estimation error remained approximately 21%. '),
    ('Practical significance: ', 'The results quantify the accuracy and current-loading tradeoff associated with the use of parameter estimates. The observed advantages apply to the two investigated disturbance cases at a reference speed of 150 rad/s. Application to auxiliary transport drives requires validation with a specific machine and mechanical load.'),
]


